      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Agustinus Kristiadi\nWestern University and Vector Institute, Canada\nhttps://agustinus.kristia.de\nAbstract\nDecision theories offer principled methods for making choices under various\ntypes of uncertainty. Algorithms that implement these theories have been success-\nfully applied to a wide range of real-world problems, including materials and drug\ndiscovery. Indeed, they are desirable since they can adaptively gather information to\nmake better decisions in the future, resulting in data-efficient workflows. In scien-\ntific discovery, where experiments are costly, these algorithms can thus signific",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Author, abstract and the opening of Chapter 1. The monograph by Agustinus Kristiadi (Western University and Vector Institute) aims to give an accessible, self-contained introduction to the theoretical analysis of probabilistic decision-making algorithms (bandits, Bayesian optimization and tree search), which adaptively gather information and so suit costly experimentation such as materials and drug discovery; it assumes only basic probability, statistics and some Gaussian process background. Chapter 1, opening with an epigraph from Hacking on frequentist and Bayesian probability, sets up decision theory: data D and a latent parameter f with an unknown joint distribution, a set of actions A, and a utility u(f, a), illustrated by symptoms, a disease and candidate drugs. It takes both Bayesian and frequentist views as valid for different purposes.",
