      "target": "../sections/sec-001-bayesian-decision-theory.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Bayesian statistics assumes that probabilities represent beliefs: “I am 50% sure”, “I am quite\nconfident that this will work”, etc. Decision-making under this framework is then used to take\nan action based on one’s (e.g., a model’s) belief about the unknown. Indeed, Bayesian decision\n1An alternative view, common in the frequentist formalism, is to replace the utility function with a loss function,\nwhich, w.l.o.g., can be taken as −𝑢.\n1\n1 Decision-Making\ntheory assumes that 𝑓is unknown and D is observed, and concerns in finding the best action 𝑎∗\nunder the posterior belief 𝑝( 𝑓| D) and the",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Bayesian decision theory. Probabilities represent beliefs; f is unknown and D observed, and the best action maximises expected utility under the posterior, a* = argmax over a of E over f ~ p(f | D) of u(f, a) (Eq. 1.1). A footnote notes the frequentist convention of a loss, taken as −u. Examples: a doctor prescribing the drug with highest expected utility under their posterior belief, the utility encoding their risk attitude; and Pascal's Wager, where infinite utilities for belief if God exists make believing optimal however small the posterior probability, with the remark that different utilities give different optimal actions.",
