      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Frequentist decision theory",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 5,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-frequentist-decision-theory.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Frequentist statistics assumes that probabilities represent long-running relative frequency: “What\nis the occurrence of a disease in a given population?”, “The error rate of this program is 1%”,\netc. That is, if we sample the data again and again, what is the proportion of a case of interest?\nNotice that, here, the data is therefore assumed to be random.\nThus, in contrast to the previous section, frequentist decision theory assumes that 𝑓, while\nstill unknown, is fixed and D is generated through D ∼𝑝(D | 𝑓). Notice that, here, the roles of\nD and 𝑓are reversed compared to their roles in the",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Frequentist decision theory and the start of Chapter 2. Probabilities are long-run relative frequencies; f is fixed but unknown and D ~ p(D | f) is random, so one seeks a decision rule δ mapping data to actions that maximises expected utility, or minimises expected loss ℓ = −u (Eqs. 1.2 and 1.3). Because this depends on the unknown f, the risk R(f, δ) = E over D of ℓ(f, δ(D)) is defined and the minimax rule minimises the worst-case risk over f (Eq. 1.4), illustrated by a public-health treatment guideline that must work for cold, malaria or COVID-19 without being best for each. Remark 1.6 answers \"Bayesian or frequentist?\" with both, as tools for different goals.",
