      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Let 𝑋be a random variable (r.v.). It is often useful to know how such a r.v. concentrated around\na value. Concentration inequalities are the bread-and-butter for theoretical analyses in machine\nlearning (and other fields!).\nExample 2.1. Let 𝑋indicate the grade of a random student in a given population (e.g. in a class).\nA concentration inequality is useful to answer the following question: “What is the prevalence\nof students with a grade at least (e.g.) 80?”\nLet P denote the long-running relative frequency of its random event argument. That is, let\n(𝑋𝑖)𝑛\n𝑖=1 be a sequence of samples of \ud835",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Concentration inequalities (Chapter 2). Probability is used here as long-run relative frequency, distinct from Bayesian belief, and the standard results are stated without proof and illustrated with student grades. Markov's inequality bounds P(X ≥ a) by E(X)/a for nonnegative X; Chebyshev's bounds deviations from the mean by Var(X)/a²; the Chernoff bound for sums of independent Bernoulli variables gives exp(−E(X)δ²/3) and exp(−E(X)δ²/2) for upper and lower deviations, and its general form uses the moment-generating function; Hoeffding's inequality bounds the deviation of a sample mean of bounded i.i.d. variables by 2 exp(−2na²/(h − l)²). Example 2.9 compares them on n fair coin tosses and the event of at least 3n/4 heads: Markov gives the constant 2/3, Chebyshev 4/n, and Chernoff exp(−n/24).",
