    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-2508.21620v2/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Explore-Then-Exploit",
      "pageStart": 10,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-explore-then-exploit.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The simplest algorithm is to explore for 𝑁𝐾< 𝑇rounds and exploit for the remaining 𝑇−𝑁\nrounds (Lattimore and Szepesvári, 2020). Exploration here means that we try each action 𝑁\ntimes. Meanwhile, exploitation means that we use our estimate of the expected reward of each\naction, ˆ𝜇(𝑎) = 1/𝑁P𝑁\n𝑡=1 𝑟𝑡(𝑎), to pick our estimate of best action ˆ𝑎∗= argmax𝑎∈A ˆ𝜇(𝑎), and\nalways pick this action. The algorithm is summarized in Algorithm 1.\nTheorem 3.1. With 𝑁= (𝑇/𝐾)2/3(log𝑇)1/3, the explore-then-exploit algorithm has regret of\nE(𝑅𝑇) ≤O\n\u0010\n(𝐾𝑇2 log𝑇)1/3\u0011\nwith probability ≥1 −2𝐾",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explore-then-exploit (Algorithm 1). Each of the K actions is tried N times, the action with the highest sample-mean reward is then chosen for the remaining rounds. Theorem 3.1 states that with N = (T/K)^(2/3)(log T)^(1/3) the expected regret is O((KT² log T)^(1/3)) with probability at least 1 − 2K/T⁴, so the algorithm has no regret with high probability. The proof defines the event E that every sample mean is within ε = √(2 log T / N) of its true mean, shows that under E each exploitation step costs at most 2ε and exploration at most NK, substitutes N, and bounds P(E) with Hoeffding's inequality and a union bound over actions. Remark 3.2 summarises the proof pattern: define a \"nice\" event, bound per-step regret under it, sum, and show the event has high probability, choosing N to tighten the bound or merely make it sublinear in T.",
