      "target": "../sections/sec-008-upper-confidence-bound-ucb.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Let us now consider the following decision rule (Auer, 2002): At each time 𝑡= 1, . . . ,𝑇, we pick\nan action that maximizes the function\nUCB𝑡(𝑎) = 𝜇𝑡(𝑎) +\n√︁\n(2 log𝑇)/𝑁𝑡(𝑎),\n(3.3)\nwhere 𝜇𝑡(𝑎) = 1/𝑁𝑡(𝑎) P𝑡\n𝑖=1 𝑟𝑖(𝑎𝑖)I(𝑎𝑖= 𝑎) is the empirical mean estimate of 𝜇(𝑎) after 𝑡\nrounds, and 𝑁𝑡(𝑎) = P𝑡\n𝑖=1 I(𝑎𝑡= 𝑎) is the number of times the action 𝑎has been selected. The\nalgorithm is summarized in Algorithm 2.\nAlgorithm 2 UCB\nInput: Time horizon 𝑇, set of 𝐾actions A\nOutput: Cumulative reward 𝑟total\n1: 𝑟total = 0\n2: for 𝑡= 1, . . . ,𝑇do\n3:\nCount 𝑁𝑡(𝑎) for e",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Upper Confidence Bound (Algorithm 2, after Auer 2002). At each step the action maximising the empirical mean plus the bonus √(2 log T / N_t(a)) is chosen, where N_t(a) counts past selections; the bonus is an error bar that encourages exploration of uncertain actions and shrinks as counts grow, leading to exploitation. Theorem 3.3 states an expected regret of O(√(Kt log T)) at each round t with probability at least 1 − 2K/T³, hence no regret with high probability. The proof shows that under the event that all estimates lie within their bonus, each gap μ(a*) − μ(a_t) is at most twice the bonus, decomposes the regret over actions, bounds the sum of √N_t(a) by √(Kt) with Jensen's inequality, and bounds the event's failure probability with Hoeffding's inequality and a union bound over actions and times.",
