      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-gaussian-processes.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Let 𝑓: X →R be a function. When 𝑋is finite, one can think of 𝑓as a collection of function\nvalues ( 𝑓(𝑥))𝑥∈X computed across evaluation/context points X. The same intuitive image can\nbe useful to think of 𝑓in the infinite case.\nA Gaussian process (GP) can be seen as a probability distribution on a function space\nH = { 𝑓: X →R} (Williams and Rasmussen, 2006). The defining property of a GP is that any\nfinite collection of evaluation points (𝑥𝑖)𝑛\n𝑖=1 ⊂X, the probability distribution over ( 𝑓(𝑥𝑖))𝑛\n𝑖=1 is\nmultivariate Gaussian. A GP is fully characterized by its mean function 𝜇: X",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gaussian processes (Chapter 4). A GP is a probability distribution over functions f: X → R such that the values at any finite set of points are jointly Gaussian; it is determined by a mean function and a covariance function given by a symmetric positive-definite kernel, equivalently one whose Gram matrix K is positive semi-definite for every finite set of points. Examples are the Matérn kernel with smoothness ν, which the text says induces a GP over functions up to k-times differentiable for k < ν (twice for ν = 5/2), and the RBF or squared-exponential kernel, the ν → ∞ limit, which induces a GP on C^∞.",
